A gear train, tumbling
======================

Three gears that actually mesh, turning at their true relative speeds, on a plate that rotates in
three dimensions — drawn by [cairo](https://github.com/sysl-lang/cairo) and shown by
[sdl3](https://github.com/sysl-lang/sdl3).

**Neither library can do this alone**, which is the whole reason this program needs both. SDL3 has a
window, an event queue and a clock, and rasterizes nothing but rectangles and textures. Cairo draws
real vector graphics — gradients, arcs, measured text, antialiasing — and has no idea what a window
is.

The join is **one buffer of ARGB32 pixels**. That is cairo's native format and one of SDL's texture
formats, and on a little-endian machine both mean the bytes B, G, R, A. So nothing is converted
anywhere and there is no per-pixel loop in this program at all:

```
cairo draws into an image surface -> surface.data() -> texture.update() -> present
```

The interesting part is the third dimension. Cairo is strictly 2D and its matrix is affine: it
rotates, scales and shears, and it cannot do perspective. That looks like it settles the question,
and it does not, for a reason worth the rest of this page.


    import sh.sysl.cairo.*
    import sh.sysl.sdl3.*
    import sysl.math.{Float, pi}
    import sysl.slices.as_ptr

    const W: int = 900
    const H: int = 560

    const WIDTH: real = 900.0
    const HEIGHT: real = 560.0

Where the assembly sits on screen. The gears are laid out about the origin, because the origin is
what the plate turns around, and this is where that origin is put.

    const SCREEN_X: real = 450.0
    const SCREEN_Y: real = 300.0


A gear, as a gear catalogue describes one
-----------------------------------------

Everything about a spur gear follows from two numbers: how many teeth it has, and the **module** $m$,
which is the size of one tooth. The **pitch circle** is where the geometry lives — the circle on
which two meshing gears roll against each other without slipping — and its radius is

$$r \;=\; \frac{m N}{2}$$

so a gear with twice the teeth is twice the size, and two gears mesh only if they were cut with the
same module. That is why the module is a constant here and not a field: a train of gears with
different modules is not a train, it is three gears near each other.

The teeth stand a distance $m$ above the pitch circle (the **addendum**) and the gaps are cut $1.25m$
below it (the **dedendum**). The extra quarter is clearance, so the tip of one gear has somewhere to
go at the bottom of the other's gap.

$$r_a = r + m \qquad r_f = r - 1.25\,m$$

    const MODULE: real = 8.0
    const ADDENDUM: real = 1.0
    const DEDENDUM: real = 1.25

How thick a gear is, and how many points one tooth of its outline is sampled at. Both are explained
where they are used.

    const THICKNESS: real = 26.0
    const PER_TOOTH: int = 8

One turn divided among the teeth is the **circular pitch** $p = 2\pi / N$, which is one tooth and one
gap together. Everything about the tooth shape below is written as a fraction of it.

    struct Gear
        teeth: int
        cx: real
        cy: real
        angle: real
        hue: real

        pitch_radius(self) -> real = MODULE * real(self.teeth) / 2.0

        tip_radius(self) -> real = self.pitch_radius() + ADDENDUM * MODULE
        root_radius(self) -> real = self.pitch_radius() - DEDENDUM * MODULE

        pitch_angle(self) -> real = 2.0 * pi / real(self.teeth)


Meshing, which is mostly a question about phase
-----------------------------------------------

Two gears mesh when their pitch circles are tangent, so the distance between their centres is

$$d \;=\; r_1 + r_2 \;=\; \frac{m\,(N_1 + N_2)}{2}$$

and rolling without slipping fixes their speeds: the arc length swept per unit time is equal at the
contact, so

$$\frac{\omega_2}{\omega_1} \;=\; -\frac{N_1}{N_2}$$

with the sign saying they turn opposite ways. Both of those are easy and neither is where the
mistakes are.

**The mistake is phase.** Two gears at exactly the right distance turning at exactly the right speeds
will still have their teeth passing through each other unless one gear's teeth line up with the
other's gaps, and there is nothing forgiving about it — an error of a few degrees is instantly
visible and looks like a broken renderer rather than like a physical impossibility.

The condition is this. Let $\beta$ be the direction from the driver's centre to the driven gear's.
At the moment a **tooth** of the driver points along $\beta$, a **gap** of the driven gear must point
back along it. Gaps sit half a pitch away from teeth, which is $\pi/N_2$, and pointing back is a
further $\pi$. Integrating the speed relation from that moment gives the driven gear's angle
outright:

$$\theta_2 \;=\; -\frac{N_1}{N_2}\,\bigl(\theta_1 - \beta\bigr) \;+\; \beta \;+\; \pi \;+\; \frac{\pi}{N_2}$$

Each term earns its place: the first rolls the gear, the second and third put the reference direction
back where the line of centres is, and the last is the half-tooth that turns a collision into a mesh.

    mesh(driver: Gear, teeth: int, bearing: real, hue: real) -> Gear
        val r = MODULE * real(teeth) / 2.0
        val d = driver.pitch_radius() + r
        val ratio = real(driver.teeth) / real(teeth)
        val half_gap = pi / real(teeth)

        Gear(teeth,
             driver.cx + d * bearing.cos(),
             driver.cy + d * bearing.sin(),
             -ratio * (driver.angle - bearing) + bearing + pi + half_gap,
             hue)
    end mesh

Everything the drawing depends on lives in one struct, so a frame is a pure function of a `Model` —
which is what makes the `--shot` picture reproducible, and what would make an export match the
window if this program had one.

    struct Model
        angle: real
        speed: real
        yaw: real
        pitch: real
        tumbling: bool
        paused: bool
        n1: int
        n2: int
        n3: int
        hover: int
        fps: real

The train: the first gear is placed by hand and the other two are placed **by meshing**, so there is
nowhere for a wrong distance to hide. Change a tooth count and the positions, the speeds and the
printed ratio all follow.

    train(m: Model) -> (Gear, Gear, Gear)
        val a = Gear(m.n1, -150.0, 40.0, m.angle, 0.55)
        val b = mesh(a, m.n2, -0.16, 0.08)
        val c = mesh(b, m.n3, -pi / 2.6, 0.33)

        (a, b, c)
    end train


The projection, and why an affine matrix is enough
--------------------------------------------------

Here is the fact the whole program rests on:

> **An orthographic projection of a *flat* object rotated in 3D is an affine map.**

A gear train is flat. So the tumble is not an approximation of a 3D rotation — it *is* one, computed
exactly, and it fits in the six numbers cairo will accept.

Take a rotation by $\theta$ about the vertical axis (yaw) followed by $\phi$ about the horizontal one
(pitch):

$$R \;=\; R_x(\phi)\,R_y(\theta) \;=\;
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos\theta & 0 & \sin\theta \\
\sin\phi\sin\theta & \cos\phi & -\sin\phi\cos\theta \\
-\cos\phi\sin\theta & \sin\phi & \cos\phi\cos\theta
\end{pmatrix}$$

An orthographic camera keeps the first two rows and throws away the third. Apply it to a point of the
plane, $(x, y, 0)$, displaced a height $z$ along the plane's normal:

$$\begin{pmatrix} u \\ v \end{pmatrix} =
\underbrace{\begin{pmatrix} \cos\theta & 0 \\ \sin\phi\sin\theta & \cos\phi \end{pmatrix}}_{\text{linear in } x,\,y}
\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix}
+ z \begin{pmatrix} \sin\theta \\ -\sin\phi\cos\theta \end{pmatrix}
+ \begin{pmatrix} S_x \\ S_y \end{pmatrix}$$

The $z$ term has no $x$ or $y$ in it. **Height above the plane contributes only to the translation** —
which is why an extrusion layer costs two subtractions here rather than a mesh and a depth buffer,
and it is the single most useful consequence of the object being flat.

Cairo writes a matrix as $u = xx\,x + xy\,y + x_0$ and $v = yx\,x + yy\,y + y_0$, so the six numbers
fall out by inspection.

    projection(m: Model, z: real) -> Matrix
        val ct = m.yaw.cos()
        val st = m.yaw.sin()
        val cp = m.pitch.cos()
        val sp = m.pitch.sin()

        Matrix(ct, sp * st, 0.0, cp, SCREEN_X + z * st, SCREEN_Y - z * sp * ct)
    end projection

Hand that to cairo and every circle becomes a true ellipse, every tooth its correct projection, and
every gradient and dash pattern comes along — for free, because that is what a transform means.

A wall of the rim, though, has corners at **two** different heights, and a matrix carries only one.
So the same projection is also written out longhand.

    project(m: Model, x: real, y: real, z: real) -> (real, real)
        val ct = m.yaw.cos()
        val st = m.yaw.sin()
        val cp = m.pitch.cos()
        val sp = m.pitch.sin()

        (SCREEN_X + ct * x + z * st, SCREEN_Y + sp * st * x + cp * y - z * sp * ct)
    end project

The row that was thrown away is not wasted: it is the **depth**, and it is what decides what is in
front of what.

$$d(\mathbf{n}) \;=\; -\cos\phi\sin\theta\;n_x \;+\; \sin\phi\;n_y \;+\; \cos\phi\cos\theta\;n_z$$

    depth_of(m: Model, nx: real, ny: real, nz: real) -> real =
        -m.pitch.cos() * m.yaw.sin() * nx + m.pitch.sin() * ny + m.pitch.cos() * m.yaw.cos() * nz

Applied to the plane's own normal $(0,0,1)$ it gives $\cos\phi\cos\theta$ — which is also the
determinant of the $2\times2$ block above. **The projection is singular exactly when the plate is
edge-on**, which is the same statement as "a plane seen edge-on has no area", and it is why the
tumble below is bounded well short of $\pm\pi/2$. Its sign says which face of the gear is the near
one.

    depth_sense(m: Model) -> real = m.pitch.cos() * m.yaw.cos()


Light
-----

A surface facing the viewer is one thing; a surface that looks solid is another. The shading is plain
Lambert — brightness is the cosine of the angle between the surface normal and the light,

$$I \;=\; \max\bigl(0,\; \hat{\mathbf{n}}_{\text{cam}} \cdot \boldsymbol{\ell}\bigr)$$

with one decision in it that matters more than the formula: **the light is fixed in camera space, not
in the world's**. It does not tumble with the assembly. A face that brightens as it turns towards the
light is the cue that says "this is a solid" far more than the thickness does; a light bolted to the
object would rotate with it and say nothing at all.

    lambert(m: Model, nx: real, ny: real, nz: real) -> real
        val ct = m.yaw.cos()
        val st = m.yaw.sin()
        val cp = m.pitch.cos()
        val sp = m.pitch.sin()

        val ex = ct * nx + st * nz
        val ey = sp * st * nx + cp * ny - sp * ct * nz
        val ez = depth_of(m, nx, ny, nz)

        val d = ex * -0.42 + ey * -0.60 + ez * -0.68

        if d < 0.0 then 0.0 else d
    end lambert


Some small things
-----------------

Text is placed by measuring it, which is the only way that does not assume a font.

    centred(cr: &Context, text: string, x: real, y: real)
        var c = cr
        val e = c.text_extents(text)

        c.move_to(x - e.width / 2.0 - e.x_bearing, y)
        c.show_text(text)
    end centred

    right_aligned(cr: &Context, text: string, x: real, y: real)
        var c = cr
        val e = c.text_extents(text)

        c.move_to(x - e.width - e.x_bearing, y)
        c.show_text(text)
    end right_aligned

One decimal place, by arithmetic rather than by a format specifier, so it is exact and needs nothing
from the standard library that might not be there.

    one_place(v: real) -> string
        val scaled = int(v * 10.0 + (if v < 0.0 then -0.5 else 0.5))
        val whole = scaled / 10
        val tenth = if scaled < 0 then -(scaled % 10) else scaled % 10
        val sign = if v < 0.0 && whole == 0 then "-" else ""

        f"$sign%s$whole%d.$tenth%d"
    end one_place

Enough colour space to keep three gears distinguishable however the tooth counts are re-cut.

    hue_rgb(h: real, v: real) -> (real, real, real)
        val i = int(h * 6.0) % 6
        val f = h * 6.0 - real(int(h * 6.0))
        val p = v * 0.35
        val q = v * (1.0 - 0.65 * f)
        val t = v * (1.0 - 0.65 * (1.0 - f))

        if i == 0 then (v, t, p)
        elif i == 1 then (q, v, p)
        elif i == 2 then (p, v, t)
        elif i == 3 then (p, q, v)
        elif i == 4 then (t, p, v)
        else (v, p, q)
    end hue_rgb


Cutting the teeth
-----------------

A real tooth flank is an **involute** of the base circle, the curve traced by unwinding a taut string
from it, and it is chosen because it keeps the contact point moving along a straight line so the
speed ratio stays exactly constant. Approximating it with a straight flank is a lie, but a small one
at this size, and it costs a page of arithmetic to fix for no visible gain here.

What does matter is the proportion. A tooth occupies **half the circular pitch** at the pitch circle,
so the tooth and the gap are equal there — that is exactly what lets any two gears of the same module
mesh. The tooth is therefore narrower at its tip and wider at its root, and the fractions below say
so: the tip land runs from $0.34p$ to $0.66p$ and the gap floor from $0.80p$ round to $0.20p$ of the
next tooth.

**The outline is a polygon rather than a path of arcs**, and that is a decision the *rim* forces:
every edge of it becomes a wall, and a wall has to know which way it faces, so the vertices are needed
one at a time. The two lands are short arcs and three points each is already smooth; the flanks are
straight and need none. Eight points to a tooth.

    outline_points(g: Gear) -> int = g.teeth * PER_TOOTH

    outline_point(g: Gear, k: int) -> (real, real)
        val fracs = [0.00, 0.10, 0.20, 0.34, 0.50, 0.66, 0.80, 0.90]
        val tip = [false, false, false, true, true, true, false, false]
        val n = outline_points(g)
        val i = ((k % n) + n) % n
        val j = i % PER_TOOTH
        val a = g.angle + (real(i / PER_TOOTH) + fracs[j]) * g.pitch_angle()
        val r = if tip[j] then g.tip_radius() else g.root_radius()

        (g.cx + r * a.cos(), g.cy + r * a.sin())
    end outline_point

The index wraps, so asking for point $k+1$ at the end of the ring is asking for point $0$, and no
caller has to think about it.

    tooth_path(cr: &Context, g: Gear)
        var c = cr
        val n = outline_points(g)
        val (x0, y0) = outline_point(g, 0)

        c.new_sub_path()
        c.move_to(x0, y0)

        for k in 1..<n
            val (x, y) = outline_point(g, k)

            c.line_to(x, y)

        c.close_path()
    end tooth_path


The holes
---------

A gear of any size is lightened by a ring of holes, and every gear has a bore for its shaft. They are
**through-holes**, and that is the whole reason they are worth this much trouble: a hole in a solid
plate is a short cylinder whose inside you can see, and painting a dark disc on the face instead is
precisely what makes a drawing look like a sticker.

The bore is the last one in the list, so a single loop covers all of them.

    hole_count(g: Gear) -> int
        val ring = if g.teeth < 14 then 0 elif g.teeth < 22 then 4 else 6

        ring + 1
    end hole_count

    hole_at(g: Gear, i: int) -> (real, real, real)
        val ring = hole_count(g) - 1
        val rr = g.root_radius()

        if i >= ring || ring == 0
            (g.cx, g.cy, rr * 0.17)
        else
            val a = g.angle + real(i) * 2.0 * pi / real(ring)

            (g.cx + rr * 0.62 * a.cos(), g.cy + rr * 0.62 * a.sin(), rr * 0.16)
    end hole_at


The face
--------

The face is the outline **with the holes taken out of it**, as one compound path filled under the
even-odd rule. A point is inside the shape when a ray from it crosses the path an odd number of
times, so a circle drawn inside the outline is a hole rather than a disc, and what was drawn before
this shows through.

That is the difference between a plate with holes in it and a plate with circles painted on it, and
it is one call to `set_fill_rule`.

    gear_face(cr: &Context, g: Gear, lit: bool, light: real)
        var c = cr
        val (r0, g0, b0) = hue_rgb(g.hue, if lit then 1.0 else 0.84)
        val r = r0 * light
        val gr = g0 * light
        val b = b0 * light
        val rp = g.pitch_radius()

        tooth_path(c, g)

        for i in 0..<hole_count(g)
            val (hx, hy, rh) = hole_at(g, i)

            c.new_sub_path()
            c.arc(hx, hy, rh, 0.0, 2.0 * pi)

        var body = radial_pattern(g.cx - rp * 0.45, g.cy - rp * 0.55, rp * 0.05,
                                  g.cx, g.cy, g.tip_radius() * 1.1)

        body.add_color_stop_rgb(0.0, r * 1.25, gr * 1.25, b * 1.25)
        body.add_color_stop_rgb(1.0, r * 0.62, gr * 0.62, b * 0.62)

        c.set_fill_rule(FillRule.EvenOdd)
        c.set_source(body)
        c.fill_preserve()
        c.set_fill_rule(FillRule.Winding)

Stroking the same compound path outlines the teeth and every hole at once — the edge where the face
meets the walls, in one call, because the path is still there after `fill_preserve`.

        c.set_source_rgba(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.45)
        c.set_line_width(1.0)
        c.stroke()

The pitch circle, dashed. It is invisible on a real gear and drawing it is what turns the picture
into a diagram: it is the circle everything above was measured from, and the place where two gears
touch always lies on both of them.

        val dashes = [3.0, 6.0]

        c.set_dash(dashes[0..<2], 0.0)
        c.set_source_rgba(1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 0.20)
        c.arc(g.cx, g.cy, rp, 0.0, 2.0 * pi)
        c.stroke()
        c.set_dash(dashes[0..<0], 0.0)

The hub is a ring of material round the bore — an annulus and not a disc, because the bore has to
stay open or the shaft has nowhere to go. Two circles under the even-odd rule again.

        val (bx, by, br) = hole_at(g, hole_count(g) - 1)

        c.new_sub_path()
        c.arc(g.cx, g.cy, g.root_radius() * 0.34, 0.0, 2.0 * pi)
        c.new_sub_path()
        c.arc(bx, by, br, 0.0, 2.0 * pi)
        c.set_fill_rule(FillRule.EvenOdd)
        c.set_source_rgba(r * 1.1, gr * 1.1, b * 1.1, 0.55)
        c.fill()
        c.set_fill_rule(FillRule.Winding)

One tooth painted, so the rotation is countable rather than merely apparent. `arc` outwards along the
tip and `arc_negative` back along the root closes the shape of a single tooth.

        val p = g.pitch_angle()

        c.new_path()
        c.arc(g.cx, g.cy, g.tip_radius(), g.angle + 0.20 * p, g.angle + 0.80 * p)
        c.arc_negative(g.cx, g.cy, g.root_radius(), g.angle + 0.80 * p, g.angle + 0.20 * p)
        c.close_path()
        c.set_source_rgba(1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 0.22)
        c.fill()
    end gear_face


The walls
---------

Here is where the matrix stops being enough. A wall joins the far face to the near one, so it has
corners at two heights, and it is drawn from projected points directly.

Two things have to be right, and together they are the whole of the hidden-surface problem in this
program.

**Cull.** The outward normal of a wall is perpendicular to its edge, and there are two candidates;
which one points outwards depends on the winding, so it is checked against the radius rather than
assumed. A wall is drawn only when it faces the viewer, $d(\mathbf{n}) < 0$. Exactly half of them
survive, and that is what lets the near face sit *on* a wall rather than inside a tube.

    rim_wall(cr: &Context, m: Model, g: Gear, k: int, r: real, gr: real, b: real, zn: real, zf: real)
        var c = cr
        val (ax, ay) = outline_point(g, k)
        val (bx, by) = outline_point(g, k + 1)
        val ex = bx - ax
        val ey = by - ay
        val len = (ex * ex + ey * ey).sqrt()

        if len > 0.0001
            var nx = ey / len
            var ny = -ex / len
            val mx = (ax + bx) / 2.0 - g.cx
            val my = (ay + by) / 2.0 - g.cy

            if nx * mx + ny * my < 0.0
                nx = -nx
                ny = -ny

            if depth_of(m, nx, ny, 0.0) < 0.0
                val shade = 0.16 + 0.62 * lambert(m, nx, ny, 0.0)
                val (p1x, p1y) = project(m, ax, ay, zf)
                val (p2x, p2y) = project(m, bx, by, zf)
                val (p3x, p3y) = project(m, bx, by, zn)
                val (p4x, p4y) = project(m, ax, ay, zn)

                c.move_to(p1x, p1y)
                c.line_to(p2x, p2y)
                c.line_to(p3x, p3y)
                c.line_to(p4x, p4y)
                c.close_path()
                c.set_source_rgb(r * shade, gr * shade, b * shade)
                c.fill()
    end rim_wall

The inside of a through-hole is the same quad with the normals pointing **inwards**, towards the
hole's axis. That one sign is the difference between seeing the near side of a tube and the far side
of it, which is what you actually see looking into a tilted hole.

A circle is convex, so its visible inner walls never overlap each other and none of the ordering
trouble below arises here. They are darker than the rim because less light reaches down a hole — the
cheapest approximation of ambient occlusion there is, and at this size it is enough.

    hole_wall(cr: &Context, m: Model, hx: real, hy: real, rh: real,
              r: real, gr: real, b: real, zn: real, zf: real)
        var c = cr
        val segs = 18

        for i in 0..<segs
            val a0 = real(i) * 2.0 * pi / real(segs)
            val a1 = real(i + 1) * 2.0 * pi / real(segs)
            val am = (a0 + a1) / 2.0
            val nx = -am.cos()
            val ny = -am.sin()

            if depth_of(m, nx, ny, 0.0) < 0.0
                val shade = 0.07 + 0.42 * lambert(m, nx, ny, 0.0)
                val ax = hx + rh * a0.cos()
                val ay = hy + rh * a0.sin()
                val bx = hx + rh * a1.cos()
                val by = hy + rh * a1.sin()
                val (p1x, p1y) = project(m, ax, ay, zf)
                val (p2x, p2y) = project(m, bx, by, zf)
                val (p3x, p3y) = project(m, bx, by, zn)
                val (p4x, p4y) = project(m, ax, ay, zn)

                c.move_to(p1x, p1y)
                c.line_to(p2x, p2y)
                c.line_to(p3x, p3y)
                c.line_to(p4x, p4y)
                c.close_path()
                c.set_source_rgb(r * shade, gr * shade, b * shade)
                c.fill()
    end hole_wall


Order, without sorting anything
-------------------------------

A gear outline is not convex — teeth stick out — so two visible walls *can* overlap in projection,
and the one in front has to be painted last. Sorting a few hundred quads every frame would work and
is not necessary, because of the shape of the depth function.

A point of the outline at polar coordinates $(\rho, \alpha)$ has depth

$$d(\alpha) \;=\; \rho(\alpha)\bigl(-\cos\phi\sin\theta\,\cos\alpha \;+\; \sin\phi\,\sin\alpha\bigr)
\;=\; \rho(\alpha)\,A\,\cos(\alpha - \alpha_0)$$

with $A = \sqrt{\cos^{2}\phi\,\sin^{2}\theta + \sin^{2}\phi}$. Ignoring the mild wobble in
$\rho(\alpha)$ between root and tip, that is **one cycle of a cosine**: a single nearest point, a
single furthest one, and monotone in between.

So the visible half is the arc centred on the nearest wall, and the two **ends** of that arc are the
far ones. Walking outwards-in from the nearest — the pair at distance $j$, then $j-1$, and so on —
paints every wall over the one behind it, in $O(n)$ and with no comparisons.

The two ends of that range are the awkward part, and are why this is not one tidy symmetric loop. At
$j = n/2$ the two sides name the **same** wall, and at $j = 0$ there is only one wall to name. Each of
those is drawn once and everything between is drawn twice — exactly $n$ walls, none missed and none
repeated.

Getting that wrong is not subtle. An earlier version of this program walked $j$ from $n/2$ down to
$1$, so the wall at $j = 0$ — the *nearest* one, in the middle of the visible band — was never drawn
at all, while $j = n/2$ was drawn twice. The result was one tooth per gear with a hole straight
through its rim, travelling round the gear as it turned.

    draw_gear(cr: &Context, m: Model, g: Gear, lit: bool)
        var c = cr
        val (r, gr, b) = hue_rgb(g.hue, if lit then 0.95 else 0.78)
        val near = if depth_sense(m) > 0.0 then -1.0 else 1.0
        val zn = near * 0.5 * THICKNESS
        val zf = -zn
        val n = outline_points(g)

        c.identity_matrix()

        var nearest = 0
        var least = 1.0e18

        for k in 0..<n
            val (ax, ay) = outline_point(g, k)
            val d = depth_of(m, ax - g.cx, ay - g.cy, 0.0)

            if d < least
                least = d
                nearest = k

        val half = n / 2

        for i in 0..<(half + 1)
            val j = half - i

            rim_wall(c, m, g, nearest + j, r, gr, b, zn, zf)

            if j > 0 && j < half
                rim_wall(c, m, g, nearest - j, r, gr, b, zn, zf)

The insides of the holes come next, before the face, because the face has them cut out of it and
these are what shows through.

        for i in 0..<hole_count(g)
            val (hx, hy, rh) = hole_at(g, i)

            hole_wall(c, m, hx, hy, rh, r, gr, b, zn, zf)

And then the near face, on top of the walls it stands on, lit by the plane's own normal — so the
whole gear dims as the assembly turns edge-on and brightens as it comes back.

        val face_light = 0.45 + 0.62 * lambert(m, 0.0, 0.0, near)

        c.save()
        c.set_matrix(projection(m, zn))
        gear_face(c, g, lit, face_light)
        c.restore()
    end draw_gear

The **pitch point** is where two meshing gears touch, at the place on the line of centres that
divides it in the ratio of the pitch radii. The whole of gear design is arranged so that this point
stands still while everything turns, which is worth marking.

    pitch_point(cr: &Context, m: Model, a: Gear, b: Gear)
        var c = cr
        val near = if depth_sense(m) > 0.0 then -1.0 else 1.0
        val t = a.pitch_radius() / (a.pitch_radius() + b.pitch_radius())
        val x = a.cx + (b.cx - a.cx) * t
        val y = a.cy + (b.cy - a.cy) * t

        c.save()
        c.set_matrix(projection(m, near * 0.5 * THICKNESS))
        c.arc(x, y, 3.5, 0.0, 2.0 * pi)
        c.set_source_rgba(1.0, 0.95, 0.75, 0.95)
        c.fill()
        c.arc(x, y, 9.0, 0.0, 2.0 * pi)
        c.set_source_rgba(1.0, 0.95, 0.75, 0.4)
        c.set_line_width(1.2)
        c.stroke()
        c.restore()
    end pitch_point


The frame
---------

    draw(context_in: &Context, m: Model)
        var cr = context_in
        val (a, b, c) = train(m)

        var sky = linear_pattern(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, HEIGHT)

        sky.add_color_stop_rgb(0.0, 0.05, 0.06, 0.10)
        sky.add_color_stop_rgb(1.0, 0.10, 0.12, 0.18)

        cr.set_source(sky)
        cr.paint()

The shafts go under everything, so they come out from behind the gears. They are in the plane, so
they get the plain projection with no height.

        cr.save()
        cr.set_matrix(projection(m, 0.0))
        cr.set_source_rgba(1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 0.09)
        cr.set_line_width(2.0)
        cr.move_to(a.cx, a.cy)
        cr.line_to(b.cx, b.cy)
        cr.line_to(c.cx, c.cy)
        cr.stroke()
        cr.restore()

The three gears lie in one plane and nothing in a gear train is inside anything else, so ordering
them by the depth of their centres is the whole of the between-gears hidden-surface problem.

        val sp = m.pitch.sin()
        val cs = m.pitch.cos() * m.yaw.sin()
        val da = -cs * a.cx + sp * a.cy
        val db = -cs * b.cx + sp * b.cy
        val dc = -cs * c.cx + sp * c.cy

        val order = if da >= db && da >= dc then
                if db >= dc then [1, 2, 3] else [1, 3, 2]
            elif db >= da && db >= dc then
                if da >= dc then [2, 1, 3] else [2, 3, 1]
            else
                if da >= db then [3, 1, 2] else [3, 2, 1]

        for i in 0..<3
            val which = order[i]

            if which == 1
                draw_gear(cr, m, a, m.hover == 1)
            elif which == 2
                draw_gear(cr, m, b, m.hover == 2)
            else
                draw_gear(cr, m, c, m.hover == 3)

        pitch_point(cr, m, a, b)
        pitch_point(cr, m, b, c)

The words stay flat in screen space, so the transform is thrown away first.

        cr.identity_matrix()

        cr.select_font_face("sans", FontSlant.Normal, FontWeight.Bold)
        cr.set_font_size(24.0)
        cr.set_source_rgb(0.96, 0.97, 1.0)
        cr.move_to(48.0, 52.0)
        cr.show_text("A gear train, tumbling")

        cr.select_font_face("sans", FontSlant.Italic, FontWeight.Normal)
        cr.set_font_size(12.0)
        cr.set_source_rgba(0.96, 0.97, 1.0, 0.5)
        cr.move_to(48.0, 74.0)
        cr.show_text("cairo cuts the teeth and projects the plane, SDL3 turns the crank and shows the frame")

        cr.select_font_face("sans", FontSlant.Normal, FontWeight.Normal)
        cr.set_font_size(11.0)
        cr.set_source_rgba(0.96, 0.97, 1.0, 0.35)

        val state = if m.paused then "stopped" else f"${one_place(m.fps)}%s fps"

        right_aligned(cr, state, WIDTH - 48.0, 52.0)
        right_aligned(cr, f"yaw ${one_place(m.yaw * 180.0 / pi)}%s°   pitch ${one_place(m.pitch * 180.0 / pi)}%s°",
                      WIDTH - 48.0, 70.0)

What the train does is **read off the gears** rather than kept beside them, so the numbers cannot
disagree with the picture. Note the middle gear does not appear in the ratio at all: an idler reverses
the direction twice and its tooth count cancels, so it sets the spacing and nothing else.

$$\frac{\omega_3}{\omega_1} \;=\; \left(-\frac{N_1}{N_2}\right)\left(-\frac{N_2}{N_3}\right) \;=\; \frac{N_1}{N_3}$$

        val out_speed = real(a.teeth) / real(c.teeth)
        val rows = [a, b, c]
        val names = ["driver", "idler", "output"]

        cr.set_font_size(12.0)

        for i in 0..<3
            val g = rows[i]
            val y = HEIGHT - 96.0 + real(i) * 22.0
            val (rr, gg, bb) = hue_rgb(g.hue, 0.95)

            cr.arc(58.0, y - 4.0, 5.0, 0.0, 2.0 * pi)
            cr.set_source_rgb(rr, gg, bb)
            cr.fill()

            cr.set_source_rgba(0.96, 0.97, 1.0, 0.72)
            cr.move_to(74.0, y)
            cr.show_text(f"${names[i]}%s — ${g.teeth}%d teeth, pitch radius ${one_place(g.pitch_radius())}%s")

        cr.set_source_rgba(0.96, 0.97, 1.0, 0.5)
        right_aligned(cr, f"the output turns ${one_place(out_speed)}%s times per turn of the driver",
                      WIDTH - 48.0, HEIGHT - 96.0)
        right_aligned(cr, "the idler sets the spacing and not the ratio — it cancels out",
                      WIDTH - 48.0, HEIGHT - 74.0)

        cr.set_font_size(11.0)
        cr.set_source_rgba(0.96, 0.97, 1.0, 0.26)
        cr.move_to(48.0, HEIGHT - 22.0)
        cr.show_text("space  stop        [ ]  speed        T  hold the tumble        R  re-cut        Q  quit")

        right_aligned(cr, f"sh.sysl.cairo ${version()}%s + sh.sysl.sdl3", WIDTH - 48.0, HEIGHT - 22.0)
    end draw


Pointing at a gear
------------------

The pointer arrives in screen coordinates and the gears live in the plane. Rather than transforming
three circles forward — which under a tumble are ellipses, and testing a point against an ellipse is
work — the pointer is put **backwards through the same matrix**. One inversion, and then the test is
three distances against three radii, exactly as it would be with no rotation at all.

This is the second dividend of the projection being affine. An affine map is invertible whenever its
determinant is non-zero, and $\det = \cos\phi\cos\theta$ is the same quantity that says the plate is
not edge-on. When it is, cairo reports `STATUS_INVALID_MATRIX` and the answer is simply "no gear",
which is correct: there is nothing to point at.

    over(m: Model, x: real, y: real) -> int
        var inv = projection(m, 0.0)

        if !inv.invert().ok()
            return 0

        val (px, py) = inv.transform_point(x, y)
        val (a, b, c) = train(m)
        val da = ((px - a.cx) * (px - a.cx) + (py - a.cy) * (py - a.cy)).sqrt()
        val db = ((px - b.cx) * (px - b.cx) + (py - b.cy) * (py - b.cy)).sqrt()
        val dc = ((px - c.cx) * (px - c.cx) + (py - c.cy) * (py - c.cy)).sqrt()

        if da < a.tip_radius() then 1
        elif db < b.tip_radius() then 2
        elif dc < c.tip_radius() then 3
        else 0
    end over

Tooth counts worth looking at, cycled rather than drawn at random: this program has nothing to seed a
generator with, and a reproducible picture is worth more here than novelty.

    recut(step: int) -> (int, int, int)
        val sets = [[18, 30, 12], [12, 26, 18], [22, 34, 14], [16, 24, 20], [14, 32, 16]]
        val s = sets[step % 5]

        (s[0], s[1], s[2])
    end recut


The loop
--------

`--shot` draws one frame at a fixed angle under SDL's dummy video driver and saves it. Every line of
the program still runs; only the display and the clock are stood in for, and the picture is the same
every time because a frame is a pure function of one `Model`.

    main(args: []string) -> Result[unit, string]
        val offscreen = args.len > 1 && args[1] == "--shot"

        if offscreen
            set_hint(HINT_VIDEO_DRIVER, "dummy")

        if !init(INIT_VIDEO)
            return Err(f"SDL would not start: ${error()}%s")

        val opened = create_window("cairo + SDL3 — a gear train", W, H, 0)

        if opened.is_none()
            return Err(f"the window would not open: ${error()}%s")

        var win = opened.unwrap()
        val started = win.create_renderer()

        if started.is_none()
            return Err(f"the renderer would not start: ${error()}%s")

        var r = started.unwrap()

The one buffer both libraries touch. Cairo owns it; SDL only ever reads it.

        var frame = image_surface(Format.Argb32, W, H)

        if !frame.ok()
            return Err(f"the drawing surface could not be made: ${frame.status()}%s")

`PIXELFORMAT_ARGB8888` is SDL's name for the same bytes cairo calls `Format.Argb32` — B, G, R, A on a
little-endian machine. Nothing is converted between the two; the texture is uploaded straight out of
cairo's own storage every frame, which is what `TEXTUREACCESS_STREAMING` exists for.

        val made = r.create_texture(PIXELFORMAT_ARGB8888, TEXTUREACCESS_STREAMING, i32(W), i32(H))

        if made.is_none()
            return Err(f"the texture could not be made: ${error()}%s")

        var tex = made.unwrap()

        r.set_vsync(true)

        var m = Model(0.0, 0.6, 0.0, 0.0, true, false, 18, 30, 12, 0, 60.0)
        var cut = 0
        var clock = 0.0
        var running = true
        var last = ticks_ns()
        var frames = 0
        var fps_at = last
        var mx = 0.0
        var my = 0.0

        while running
            var pending = poll_event()

            while pending.is_some()
                val e = pending.unwrap()
                val kind = e.kind

                if kind == EVENT_QUIT
                    running = false
                elif kind == EVENT_MOUSE_MOTION
                    mx = real(e.mouse_x())
                    my = real(e.mouse_y())
                    m.hover = over(m, mx, my)
                elif kind == EVENT_KEY_DOWN
                    val k = e.key_code()

SDL spells an ordinary key as its own character, so these are ASCII.

                    if k == 113 || k == 27
                        running = false
                    elif k == 32
                        m.paused = !m.paused
                    elif k == 91
                        m.speed = m.speed * 0.7
                    elif k == 93
                        m.speed = m.speed * 1.4
                    elif k == 116
                        m.tumbling = !m.tumbling
                    elif k == 114
                        cut += 1

                        val (n1, n2, n3) = recut(cut)

                        m.n1 = n1
                        m.n2 = n2
                        m.n3 = n3
                        m.hover = over(m, mx, my)

                pending = poll_event()

            val now = ticks_ns()
            var dt = real(now - last) / 1.0e9

            if offscreen then dt = 1.0 / 60.0
            if dt > 0.25 then dt = 0.25

            last = now

            if !m.paused then m.angle += dt * m.speed

The tumble **swings rather than spins**. Two sines whose periods are incommensurate never come back
into step, so the assembly keeps presenting an attitude it has not shown before — and staying inside
these bounds keeps the plate well off edge-on, where $\cos\phi\cos\theta \to 0$, the projection is
singular, and there would be nothing to see anyway.

            if m.tumbling
                clock += dt

                m.yaw = 0.85 * (clock * 0.37).sin()
                m.pitch = 0.55 * (clock * 0.23).sin()
                m.hover = over(m, mx, my)

            frames += 1

            if now - fps_at > 500_000_000u64
                m.fps = real(frames) * 1.0e9 / real(now - fps_at)
                frames = 0
                fps_at = now

The frame itself, which is the whole of the join between the two libraries: cairo draws, SDL uploads
and shows.

            var cr = context(frame)

            draw(cr, m)
            frame.flush()

            tex.update(as_ptr(frame.data()), i32(frame.stride()))
            r.clear()
            r.copy(tex)
            r.present()

            if offscreen
                m.angle = 0.42
                m.yaw = 0.62
                m.pitch = 0.38
                m.hover = 2

                var shot = context(frame)

                draw(shot, m)
                frame.flush()

                if !frame.write_png("demo.png").ok()
                    return Err("the screenshot could not be written")

                print("wrote demo.png")
                running = false

        quit()
        Ok(())
    end main
